    dct:abstract "A planned vaccine-response intervention examines immune and patient-reported outcomes in community settings along the Iberian coast. This is a wholly fictional interventional research example designed to illustrate how a completed machine-actionable data management plan can be discovered alongside other study descriptions. The fictional coordinating institution is Iberian Community Vaccine Lab. Its study countries are Spain, Portugal; these locations indicate where the study takes place and do not automatically describe the geographical coverage of any future published dataset. The study is labelled not yet recruiting at the time of the example. Dates refer to the study plan, not to trial registration or metadata publication. The central scientific question concerns seasonal respiratory vaccination. The investigators compare intervention and routine-care pathways using a study protocol that specifies patient selection, follow-up and outcome measurement. The target population includes Adults, Adolescent. The description of eligibility is simplified for the demonstration, and any real version would require precise inclusion and exclusion criteria, site approvals, risk assessment and a scientifically justified recruitment strategy. The planned enrolment is 1500 and the achieved or currently recorded enrolment is 0 in this invented submission. These illustrative figures are not observed research results and should not be used for epidemiological inference. Research activities are represented through setting categories such as Community, Primary care. The maDMP records data categories including Serology, Sociodemographic and primary outcome categories including Physicological or Clinical, Adverse event. This structured metadata is useful for identifying related research, comparing study scope and noticing differences in data collection practices. It is not equivalent to access to clinical records, laboratory measurements or individual participant information. The plan focuses primarily on newly collected observations rather than reuse of external participant-level sources; this does not remove the need to document provenance and data quality. A real investigator would also describe missing-data management, audit trails, versioning, documentation and responsible stewardship at each participating institution. The completed form reports standards such as CDISC CDASH/SDTM, MedDRA. Selecting these names means the researcher says they use or plan to use them; conformance cannot be established from a checkbox alone. The investigators report an intention to share participant-level data in the future, subject to consent, ethics, safeguards and access agreements. Data access questions must be considered separately from study discovery because consent language, retention periods, transfer limitations and data access committees can impose additional requirements. In this demonstration, the purpose of collecting the maDMP is to enable transparent searching for studies and potential collaborators, while preserving enough context to ask informed questions before any data-sharing negotiation. All people, identifiers, funders and organisations described here are fabricated for safe testing." ;
