<https://example.org/comect-demo/study/SYN-0008> a ex:Study ;
    dct:abstract "A school-based intervention evaluation compares environmental and respiratory indicators before and after prevention measures. This is a wholly fictional interventional research example designed to illustrate how a completed machine-actionable data management plan can be discovered alongside other study descriptions. The fictional coordinating institution is Atlantic Community Prevention Lab. Its study countries are Ireland, United Kingdom; these locations indicate where the study takes place and do not automatically describe the geographical coverage of any future published dataset. The study is labelled recruiting at the time of the example. Dates refer to the study plan, not to trial registration or metadata publication. The central scientific question concerns ventilation and hygiene interventions in schools. The investigators compare intervention and routine-care pathways using a study protocol that specifies patient selection, follow-up and outcome measurement. The target population includes Children (1 year, Adolescent. The description of eligibility is simplified for the demonstration, and any real version would require precise inclusion and exclusion criteria, site approvals, risk assessment and a scientifically justified recruitment strategy. The planned enrolment is 4200 and the achieved or currently recorded enrolment is 1700 in this invented submission. These illustrative figures are not observed research results and should not be used for epidemiological inference. Research activities are represented through setting categories such as School, Community. The maDMP records data categories including Environmental, Sociodemographic and primary outcome categories including Physicological or Clinical, Resource use. This structured metadata is useful for identifying related research, comparing study scope and noticing differences in data collection practices. It is not equivalent to access to clinical records, laboratory measurements or individual participant information. The plan focuses primarily on newly collected observations rather than reuse of external participant-level sources; this does not remove the need to document provenance and data quality. A real investigator would also describe missing-data management, audit trails, versioning, documentation and responsible stewardship at each participating institution. The completed form reports standards such as LOINC, HL7 FHIR. Selecting these names means the researcher says they use or plan to use them; conformance cannot be established from a checkbox alone. The reported intention is that de-identified participant-level data are already shared in the fictional scenario, but access conditions still have to be reviewed and approval cannot be assumed. Data access questions must be considered separately from study discovery because consent language, retention periods, transfer limitations and data access committees can impose additional requirements. In this demonstration, the purpose of collecting the maDMP is to enable transparent searching for studies and potential collaborators, while preserving enough context to ask informed questions before any data-sharing negotiation. All people, identifiers, funders and organisations described here are fabricated for safe testing." ;
